  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nUpper level ridging and surface troughing in the lee of the \nRockies will result in warm temperatures through at least\nSaturday, with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s from northeast\nto southwest. By Saturday the ridge will move off to the east of\nthe high plains, allowing low to mid level moisture to advance \nnorthward from the tropics onto the western high plains. \nWidespread showers are expected to form Sunday morning, with \nperhaps a break in the early afternoon. Given some mid level \ncold advection, there may be enough instability for strong to \nmarginally severe t-storms Sunday afternoon in far western \nKansas. However, cloud cover, cool temperatures and poor lapse \nrates will likely preclude severe weather altogether.\n\nRain amounts and timing will depend on how fast the upper trough \nmoves east, with a slower solution possibly resulting in locally\nheavy rains up to 2\" across western Kansas. The various \nensemble suites vary on the speed of the system and thus on \nthe duration and amounts of rain. The grand ensemble indicates \n40-70% chances for 1+\" of rain across far western Kansas (US-83 \nand west to the state line) and similar chances of .75\" from\nDodge City to Hays. South central Kansas will likely get the \nleast amount of rain, including locations such as Pratt and \nMedicine Lodge, with moderate to high chances of .5\" or greater.\n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough will weaken after passing\nthe high plains, with the associated cool frontal passage \nMonday morning. Expect highs in the 50s and 60s Monday. This \ncooler air is forecast to linger into Tuesday.",
